e piece just seeing  what kind of stuff i could come up with  and eventually i kind of came upon an  old riff i had made for our band to keep  and it just kind of seemed to fit the  feel and the tone of it it was very it  was very heavy it was very melodic and  it uh it had a lot of good moments for  kind of soloing and guitar wankery so  for recording the piece i would play  guitar and bass and then i would use a  midi controller to play a sample drum  kit all these instruments i'd be running  through a lot of native instruments  plug-ins such as guitar rig and contact  so here's a couple tips on getting your  instruments to sound great firstly it's  all about layering almost every single  instrument i have playing in the song  has some sort of companion layer that  gives it a little extra kick and um for  drums i start with a normal kit i'm  playing on a midi controller i duplicate  the bass and snare and have those run  through like an 80s drum type sounding  sample and for the guitar one great  trick i like doing is offsetting the  layers a little bit that way you get a  really great stereo sound that really  brings the richness and fullness out of  it here's a quick run through of all the  layers i have going in the music i start  with the bass here which is the drum and  the kick  then i add real drums on top of that  [Music]  the bass comes in and follows the drums  very strongly  as well as the rhythm guitar  then i put the lead on top of that to  play the main melody  [Music]  now you'll notice that when i play each  section of the song you can still hear  the rest playing very clearly that's  because of mixing when mixing a song you  have to recognize that every single  instrument occupies a very specific  space in the mix for drums you have to  kick in the low end and the snare and  cymbals in the upper mids to highs i  like the bass guitar even lower than the  drums with a little bit of mids for  definition and so it doesn't compete i  bring down the lows and raise the mids  for the rhythm guitar and then for the  lead guitar i do the same while boosting  the highs even more  the key to this is that by not having  all your instruments overlapping and  occupying the same frequency your ears  are able to distinguish each one from  each other and brings the song a lot of  clarity that sometimes you wouldn't  otherwise get so i hope you guys learned  something from this video i'm also  uploading a multi-track of this song to  our server so you can download it and  listen to all the tracks that i used in  this song also if you haven't seen it  yet i've uploaded a guitar tab for this  song if you want to use the multi-track  and record your own version of it we'd  love to see what you guys come up with  if you guys have any questions of course  leave them in the comments box or send  us a tweet at  corridor digital right in front of my  face here nice and perfect all right  guys that's all  laters  [Music]  oh
 

